**Event loop rules.** Synchronous code runs first on the call stack. Then **all microtasks** (promise callbacks, `await` continuations) run. Then one **macrotask** (timer callback) at a time, with microtasks drained between each. Timers fire in order of their delay.

**Part 1 (reported snippet).** Synchronous pass: `A`, `D`, `E` (the promise executor runs synchronously), `G`, then `callAsyncFunc()` runs and calls `asyncFunc()`, which logs `I` synchronously. Microtasks: `F` was queued first, then the continuation after `await asyncFunc()` which logs `J`. Timers: `B` (0 ms), `H` (10 ms), `C` (100 ms).
**Output:** `A D E G I F J B H C`.

**Part 2.** `exampleAsyncFunctionOne` awaits the 3 s promise, then **creates** the 6 s one, so it takes about **9 s**. `exampleAsyncFunctionTwo` starts both timers immediately, so the awaits overlap and total is about **6 s**. Both return the two result strings in order; times are printed as roughly 9000 and 6000 ms. Lesson: use `Promise.all` or start promises before awaiting when tasks are independent.

**Part 3.** `add({val:100})` returns `obj1.val + 100 = 200`. `obj` and `obj1` are the **same object reference** and `add` closes over `obj1`, so after `obj.val = -100` the second call returns `0`.

**promiseAllSync without async/await.**
```js
function promiseAllSync(promises) {
  return new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
    const results = new Array(promises.length);
    let done = 0;
    if (!promises.length) return resolve(results);
    promises.forEach((p, i) => {
      Promise.resolve(p).then(v => {
        results[i] = v;
        if (++done === promises.length) resolve(results);
      }, reject);
    });
  });
}
```
It preserves order using the index, rejects on the first failure, and handles an empty array. For **sequential** resolution, chain with `reduce`: `promises.reduce((acc, p) => acc.then(r => p.then(v => [...r, v])), Promise.resolve([]))`. Note a promise already started runs regardless; true sequential execution needs functions returning promises.
